{
  "title": "Japan Weather in June 2026: Embrace the Lush Greenery ...",
  "url": "https://www.umetravel.com/japan-weather/weather-in-june.html",
  "snippet": "Temperature: 55°F–70°F (13°C–21°C).\n\nIf you ask me where to escape the humidity that creeps into the rest of Asia in June, I'll always say Hokkaido. While Tokyo and Kyoto embrace their \"moody rain\" phase, Hokkaido skips the rainy season (tsuyu) entirely. I call it the \"Golden Window.\"\n\nCompared to May, when it can still feel brisk with lingering snow on the peaks, June finds Hokkaido in full, vibrant bloom. The air is incredibly dry and clear—so much so that the locals call it the \"Sapporo Blue\" season because the sky looks impossibly deep blue. Instead of the sweltering 80-degree weather of July, you'll enjoy perfect, light-jacket weather that makes you want to be outside all day. [...] ### Facts about June Weather in Japan 2026\n\nMild Temperatures: Expect daily highs ranging from 72°F to 82°F (22°C to 28°C). While it’s comfortably warm, the increasing humidity makes it feel like summer is just around the corner.\n\nThe \"Plum Rain\" Season: Most of Japan (except Hokkaido) enters the rainy season in June. However, don't let the word \"rainy\" discourage you! Rainfall is often intermittent rather than constant, leaving plenty of time for exploration.\n\nRegional Variations:\n\nTokyo and Kyoto: Expect moderate humidity with occasional afternoon showers.\n\nHokkaido: The \"North Star\" of Japan is the driest and coolest region, perfect for those looking to escape dampness.\n\nOkinawa: The rainy season usually ends here by mid-June, welcoming bright sunshine and beach-perfect weather. [...] High humidity: As the month progresses, humidity levels rise. We recommend packing breathable, moisture-wicking fabrics to stay comfortable during city walks.\n\n### Regional Weather Breakdown: Where to Go in June\n\n#### Central Japan: The Atmospheric Heart (Tokyo, Kyoto, Osaka, Kanazawa)\n\nThe Vibe: Misty, poetic, and undeniably \"Ghibli.\"\n\nTemperature: 75°F to 82°F (24°C to 28°C)"
}
{
  "title": "Japan Weather in June 2026: How to Enjoy Japan during the Rainy Month",
  "url": "https://www.japanhighlights.com/japan/weather-in-june",
  "snippet": "In Hokkaido and mountainous areas of Japan (Takayama and Kamikochi), the weather is cooler. Hokkaido, in particular, is the coolest region in Japan during June, with highs reaching only 21°C (70°F).\n\nIt doesn't rain every day in June, and showers or light rain are common. More rain falls in late June. When it does rain, you can expect a pleasant coolness, making it perfect to wear a long-sleeved shirt over a T-shirt.\n\n## Tokyo and Central Japan Weather in June: Not too Hot\n\nOur clients in TokyoOur clients in Tokyo\n\nYou'll expect muggy weather in central Japan cities like Tokyo, Kyoto, and Osaka. [...] I visited Japan last June and everything was perfect. The key is all in how you plan it! In this article, I'll share how to enjoy Japan better. You'll find insights on the weather, where to go, clothing, crowds, and more.\n\n## Japan's Weather and Climate in June\n\nJapan weather in June\n\nJune is the beginning of summer and the rainy season in Japan. The weather gets hotter, and rainfall becomes more frequent.\n\nCompared to July and August, June is the coolest summer month. The heat and humidity are more comfortable for you.\n\nIn central cities like Tokyo, Kyoto, and Osaka, it's not too hot yet. Temperatures range between 19-26°C (67-79°F). After rain, you'll feel muggy as the humidity increases. [...] In June, this region isn't too hot yet. Temperatures usually range between 19-26°C (67-79°F), with occasional highs of 30°C (86°F). However, humidity can reach up to 75%, making it feel hotter than it is. A T-shirt is perfect for this weather.\n\nThe rainy season begins this month. It doesn't rain every day, and when it does, it's usually just light showers. This means you'll have plenty of dry time to explore the attractions of these cities. If you prefer more sunny days, visiting in early June is a better option.\n\nThe rainy season (June to September) makes it the worst time to view Mount Fuji from a distance. Due to the rain, visibility of the mountain is at its lowest and is often hidden by clouds."
}